<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Museo di Capodimonte</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1738</span> · Also known as Capodimonte Museum, National Museum of Capodimonte</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Museo di Capodimonte is an art museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, housed in the Royal Palace of Capodimonte, begun in 1738 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles of Bourbon</a> to display the Farnese collection he had inherited from his mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisabeth-farnese">Elisabeth Farnese</a>. Its holdings include paintings by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parmigianino">Parmigianino</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravaggio">Caravaggio</a>, while the Farnese classical sculptures went to the National Archaeological Museum of Naples.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/museo-di-capodimonte.jpg" alt="Museo di Capodimonte" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: IlSistemone · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ReggiaCapodimonte.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
